{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck","id":1403866,"title":"Making Quietus not suck","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2020-07-21T12:46:40-07:00","id":1403866,"title":"Making Quietus not suck","content":"In V20, It does cool things, but it does not satisfy. The mechanics of powers beyond the second are sorely lacking. <br /> <br /> <b>Silence of Death: </b><br /> Mechanically, the only complaint here is that V20's lore of the clans came up with cool Vizier/Sorcerer improvements to the power that are objectively better than the standard (warrior) version. <br /> Fluff wise; It fits the -cool assassin- vibe but there's a disconnect as literally every other power is about turning your blood into various poisons, so one could only explain away this power as POISONING SOUND. <br /> <br /> <b>Scorpion's touch</b>:<br /> Cool power. It should be clarified if stamina is lost before or after damage done with a weapon, however, or how long the poison may last (indefinitely if I keep it in my body and don't fill up on blood?) <br /> The DAV20 version is has a good amount of complexity and is nicely detailed. However, It's<i> absurdly strong</i>. <br /> <br /> <b>Dagon's call. </b><br /> Here's where Quietus becomes a bad investment. <br /> For vampires, this doesn't work well unless your victim is a loser with terrible willpower (in which case easier methods of dispatching such a foe are almost surely available) and for humans it works well but... wouldn't mundane poison suffice? <br /> <br /> So the solutions to Dagon's call I thought of: <br /> <br /> - Everything works the same, but make the damage Aggravated. (that said, you should be able to incapacatate a vampire foe risking their final death) <br /> -Everything works the same, but the difficulty to attack is capped at 6 and the difficulty to defend is capped at 9<br /> <s>- The Victim rolls stamina+fortitude, the attacker rolls stamina+Quietus, both at difficulty 6</s> Way too powerful. <br /> - The victim rolls stamina+fortitude against a difficulty of the attacker's permanent willpower (maximum 9), the attacker rolls stamina. <br /> -<s>Both sides contest stamina, difficulty of the enemy's willpower. However, each dot of fortitude the defender possesses reduces the defence's difficulty, and each dot of quietus reduces the attack difficulty to a minimum of 4. (If Alan the Assamite Attacker had 3 quietus and his opponent had 7 willpower, he would roll his stamina at difficulty 4 (7-3=4) , and If Vincent the Ventrue Victim had 4 Fortitude and Alan had 9 willpower, Vincent would roll his stamina at a difficulty of 5 (9-4=5) ) </s><br /> -Both roll stamina, the base difficulty is 6: This difficulty is increased for the attacker per dot of victim fortitude quietus, while the attack difficulty is decreased by 1 per dot of quietus beyond the fist two levels. (someone with quietus 3 attacking an opponent without fortitude does so at difficulty 5) <br /> -Both roll stamina, the base difficulty is 6, The victim adds their Fortitude minus the attacker's Quietus rating to their stamina pool (A higher quietus than fortitude does nothing) <br /> <s>-Both roll willpower; Each success determines the difficulty of enemy's stamina roll. </s> Too finicky. <br /> -Both roll stamina, the difficulty is the enemy's willpower, but the attacker reduces the difficulty by their quietus and the defender reduces the difficulty by their fortitude; Minimum 3, maximum 9. <br /> <br /> <br /> I think the first (It's simple and works well on vampires without becoming worse for humans) is pretty good. I had lots of ideas (some so shit I wouldn't even write em out) <br /> I feel Fortitude should be relevant in this roll, but I also don't want to make things too complicated or break things for other splats, and one must also keep in mind the synergy of Scorpion's touch... <br /> (Fortitude can of course be substituted by another splat's equivalent or whatever's relevant, <i>R</i><i>esist Toxin</i> should be a good boon. )<br /> <br /> <b>Baal's Caress</b><br /> perhaps a lawyer could point out there's no restriction on what kind of weapons you can use this on and win, but otherwise it's a level 2 power at level 4. <br /> <b>Taste of Death: </b><br /> Also total shit. You can spit some acid. 2 dice of aggravated damage per blood spent (no per-turn wavier, so this might be effective when you're 7th gen) <br /> <br /> Now, I looked at DAV20. It did a smart thing by putting these two powers as one power. The Caveat there is that this power is <i>absurdly powerful</i>. Like, If you're going to make an Assamite, you're going to give yourself high willpower, and +4 or +5 damage added on to your now-aggravated +X weapon and it's carryover and your strength.... You should probably need a higher level power if you wanted to take on God like that. <br /> <br /> So, I'm thinking how can I make this level 4 power of aggravated acid poison good and balanced? <br /> <br /> <b>Baal's Caress (possibly a good version) </b><br /> One blood point is converted into acid (normal per turn expenditure applies) <br /> When applied to a a brawl or melee weapon (you may coat your hands), you roll willpower (difficulty 6) and your successes give you a number of strikes (or turns grappling) that do +1 aggravated damage. <br /> You may instead leave the Ichor in your blood system, coat an object to be used as a trap, spit the poison, or otherwise leave the poison lying around. Should a victim make contact with the poison, roll willpower: Each success grants a turn where that poison does one level of aggravated damage. Like fire, A maximum of three levels of aggravated damage occurs per turn depending on exposure (A maximum of 5 damage per turn per points the victim ingested) The difficulty to soak is 8 when delivered this manner. One point of blood turned to acid effects one person; If a person touches multiple points of poison in a single turn without increasing the area of exposure (IE, touching two points worth of the ichor with one hand) only one point effects him (The other remains active and awaits either a new part of the body, a second person, or for the turn to pass) If an infected hand still burning from the ichor touches more poison in a latter turn, that poison may increase the duration (Roll the Assamite's willpower again) <br /> <br /> Finally, if you apply this poison to a ranged weapon, or you decide to stab a man with a poison weapon and leave the weapon in him, the initial strike does standard aggravated damage (normal soak difficulties) and the projectile or weapon's poison will follow the system of non-strike poison : It will cause damage at the rate of one level per turn, difficulty 8 to soak, maximum of three damage per turn, until the successes on the willpower roll have been used up. Firearms cannot deliver this poison as the heat destroys it. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Therein, by merging powers 4 and 5 together, we have a <b>level 5 </b>power to work out... <br /> Quicken the Mortal blood from DAV20 is absurd. We need a new power. <br /> <br /> Lazy Idea 1: <br /> <b>Blood of destruction</b>. <br /> Super acid poison, like, difficulty 9 to soak, destroys inaminate objects with ease; Carve through safes and warship hulls with this power. Something to that effect. I'm lazy describing it, but I can imagine it'd be pretty versatile. Perhaps it destroys precisely what you want and leaves other stuff untouched. <br /> <br /> Idea 2: <br /> <b>Poison the Well</b><br /> Your blood, or the poison of any quietus power you know, goes into a mortal without effecting them at all. Anyone who drinks from that mortal suffers the consequences, be it a blood bond, Scorpion's touch, Baal's Caress, you may launch Dagon's call at will on anyone who touches the mortal; All of this as if you were the one there. (your stamina and willpower is used, not the mortal's) This power would be the nightmare of all Ventrue. (There was a level 6 power that did almost this I think) <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So<br /> Thoughts? Prayers? How would you all fix Quietus?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-21T15:04:05-07:00","id":1403890,"title":"","content":"The issue with Scorpion's Touch in DAV:20 is that it allows a roll, think it was wits+survival, to realize they've been poisoned. Passing the roll lets them spend a blood to completely negate the poison. In play, nobody ever failed that roll, so the power was completely useless. Either that roll to realize you've been poisoned needs to be higher difficulty or weaken the effects and completely do away with the resistance roll.<br /> <br /> Dagon's Call in V20 doesn't seem as bad, so long as you've got decent stamina and willpower, since it's a contested roll. You could probably pull it off way more often than DAV:20's Scorpion's Touch. It could be souped up a little, bit, though. Still feels like it should be swapped with Baal's Caress for a 3rd level power, though.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14519-drhappyangry","name":"DrHappyAngry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14519_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-21T15:12:27-07:00","id":1403891,"title":"","content":"Random musings:<br /> <br /> So, my biggest issues with Quietus have always been that the first power has no thematic connection to the others (as mentioned), and that the powers feel\u2026redundant. Turn your blood into poison, turn your blood into <i>better</i> poison, turn your blood into more versatile poison\u2026<br /> <br /> Some other clan-specific disciplines have the same issues (like Vicissitude, which is three powers that all do the same thing to different extents, and two unrelated Protean powers). And I'm not sure it's something that can be entirely fixed within V20's paradigm. Maybe a good model would be Chimerstry, which does a good job of making the enhancements feel meaningful, imo: each one feels like a significant upgrade over Ignis Fatuus in and of itself, but comes with a significant cost that means you won't want to use it every time. But &quot;poisonous blood&quot; is a very narrow space to work within.<br /> <br /> Maybe borrow some ideas from the Tremere's Path of Blood? &quot;Manipulate properties of vitae&quot; is a larger thematic space to explore than &quot;your blood is poison&quot;. Take some of the elder abilities, like &quot;inflict the drawbacks of high Generation on someone&quot;, and move them down into the easily-accessible 1-5 progression; mix them with variants on some of the Tremere abilities, like stealing vitae from a distance, which could be an enhancement to Dagon's Call. And after all, the Banu Haqim were following the Path of Blood for thousands of years before the Tremere showed up. (That last one is a joke, if it wasn't clear.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24808-draconis","name":"Draconis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar24808_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"805"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-21T16:05:34-07:00","id":1403903,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>DrHappyAngry</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1403890#post1403890\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The issue with Scorpion's Touch in DAV:20 is that it allows a roll, think it was wits+survival, to realize they've been poisoned. Passing the roll lets them spend a blood to completely negate the poison. In play, nobody ever failed that roll, </div> </div> </div> </div>Ah, thanks for pointing that out. It's a wits+alertness roll. Sounds pretty shit. I guess you could force someone to spend a blood every turn.... yeah it's pretty bad for vampiresand ghouls. However, it's extremely strong for humans. Still, the modern version is pretty good. <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Dagon's Call in V20 doesn't seem as bad, so long as you've got decent stamina and willpower, since it's a contested roll. You could probably pull it off way more often than DAV:20's Scorpion's Touch. It could be souped up a little, bit, though. Still feels like it should be swapped with Baal's Caress for a 3rd level power, though. </div> </div> </div>The only difference of note was that you got up-to an hour in VTM and you could do any amount of time in DAV20 (The fluff implies you need to wait at least an hour but the rules say otherwise) and you made an awareness roll to get in contact with the victim. <br /> <br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24808-draconis\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar24808_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"24808\" data-vbnamecard=\"24808\">Draconis</a>. <br /> The first three powers of Vicissitude, to me, are a very logical progression and they don't make the others redundant. 4 and 5 have a lot of squandered potential to them. I do think Quietus could do a lot with poisonous blood: just think about all the drugs, real world and fictional, that you've heard of. It's surprising that before LotC the discipline has been limited to a stamina killer and dangerous acid: The mind poisons of the Vizier is something worth expanding on, as is the sorcerer -spirit poison- stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-21T16:58:45-07:00","id":1403909,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck#post1403866\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In V20, It does cool things, but it does not satisfy. The mechanics of powers beyond the second are sorely lacking.<br /> <br /> <b>Silence of Death: </b><br /> Mechanically, the only complaint here is that V20's lore of the clans came up with cool Vizier/Sorcerer improvements to the power that are objectively better than the standard (warrior) version.<br /> Fluff wise; It fits the -cool assassin- vibe but there's a disconnect as literally every other power is about turning your blood into various poisons, so one could only explain away this power as POISONING SOUND.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oddly enough the ability to control sound in an area became rather game breaking the times I ran Assamite coterie games. But I believe that was dependent on the characters used and the real life knowledge of the players.<br /> <br /> For example a ex-military sniper with this ability had a habit of hunting werewolf packs. An entirely silent anti-material rifle (50 cal or bigger) from over a mile away deals with most problems. Now one of the interesting things about big boy ammo of 50 cal+ is that you can do things like have an internal poison blood core in the bullet. Yes my group included a couple of people who had IRL gun smithing and shooting knowledge.<br /> <br /> The coterie in that game also made a lot of use of explosives, stealth claymore mines makes trying to attack them..... challenging. Being able to set off a series of claymores and have anyone outside the area of effect just think some one was using flash photography as long as they didn't get hit by the shrapnel caused some disconcerting game moments.<br /> <br /> As for the other abilities, the biggest issue is that they were designed to be &quot;assassiny&quot; but without being an auto win combo when you paired it with Celerity and melee combat. I always felt that the poison blood abilities should have be Serpentis powers rather than Quietus powers. In truth all the different poison abilities should just be boiled down to say one ability at rank 5 and the other levels filled in with alternatives.<br /> <br /> Regardless which powers go where, there is still the core concept that assassination is 90% preparation and 10% execution. Meaning that you will spend days, weeks, or even months doing recon, gathering intel, and making plans. Then once everything is in place, you strike and disappear into the night. Almost no game play mechanics are designed around this aspect, at best you get a hand waved auspex or obfuscate use to move things along.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-21T17:18:46-07:00","id":1403910,"title":"","content":"If quietus just kept things quiet, it would become a boring discipline<br /> <br /> also your players conned you; bullets, especially big ones like the 50.BMG, make noise as they zoom through the air; someone experienced can tell the direction of a shot from that sound. <br /> <br /> Replacing the heavy metal core of the bullet with blood would severly harm it's effectiveness, and vitae spoils... <br /> and mines... not being able to hear the explosion at your feet is a blessing, not a curse; they may take our legs, but they have saved us our hearing!!! also SoD has a range limit and being so close to mines....<br /> <br /> poison is a strong theme, as far as magic works, and nobody epse can have it. settites aren't about poison, they're about religion, why is why serpentis is godly.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-22T12:12:30-07:00","id":1404069,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1403910#post1403910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If quietus just kept things quiet, it would become a boring discipline<br /> <br /> also your players conned you; bullets, especially big ones like the 50.BMG, make noise as they zoom through the air; someone experienced can tell the direction of a shot from that sound.<br /> <br /> Replacing the heavy metal core of the bullet with blood would severly harm it's effectiveness, and vitae spoils...<br /> and mines... not being able to hear the explosion at your feet is a blessing, not a curse; they may take our legs, but they have saved us our hearing!!! also SoD has a range limit and being so close to mines....<br /> <br /> poison is a strong theme, as far as magic works, and nobody epse can have it. settites aren't about poison, they're about religion, why is why serpentis is godly.</div> </div> </div> </div>I wasn't suggesting the entire discipline be about keeping things quiet, just that how it is used matters a lot.<br /> <br /> As for the sound of a riffle shot, there are three sounds that occur when a shot is taken.<br /> 1. The report of the rifle which is the gases pushing the bullet out of the barrel<br /> 2. The sound created by the bullet itself as it goes through the air which is a function of how fast it is traveling<br /> 3. The sound of the round hitting the target.<br /> <br /> The initial rifle report is the thing which allows you to get a baring on the direction of the shot, especially if the shooter is stupid enough to fire more than once from the same position without a counter measure. This issue can be reduced by a suppressor or completely canceled by V20 Quietus 1.<br /> <br /> The speed at which the bullet travels often goes above the speed of sound, especially in 50 cal or better rounds. This means the sound you hear is a small sonic boom that you catch only as it passes your position, so it is not a directionality thing. But this has an interesting side affect, namely that a bullet will hit the target before the sound of the shot can be heard by the people next to the target, depending on range and powder load this can be 5+ seconds worth of time difference. Thus the targets head explodes and then when everyone is screaming or diving for cover the sound hits the area.<br /> <br /> Alternatively you can use sub-sonic rounds which don't hit nearly as hard or go as far but like wise don't create the mini-sonic boom sound as it flies through the air. Also if we are talking about planned shots, part of that is considering the sound levels near the target, traffic noise, a night club, a water fall, etc. So even minor bullet whistle isn't going to help figure out the shooters location.<br /> <br /> As for the heavy metal core being removed and reducing effectiveness, that only applies if you are trying to maintain the rounds armor penetrating capability. When hitting soft targets it is often more advantageous to have the round hit and tumble around inside the target than pass straight through. So if you are hunting a werewolf, using a custom lead round with silver talons and an internal blood vial is far more lethal than a standard slap round that can cut through a manhole cover.<br /> <br /> Of course there is nothing stopping a marksman from using a variety of rounds to achieve different jobs, like the first shot is a standard AP round to break a barrier or disable a vehicle, the second shot is the custom round for what ever you are hunting and the third is an incendiary round to touch off the gas tank or start a fire. As for the vitae spoiling, given the amount of planning that goes into a given shot and the distances involved it you wouldn't be using a full mag of these things, just one per target and its not like you can't just bring the press with you and assemble the blood rounds on site just before the shooting starts.<br /> <br /> As for saving your hearing, yes and no. You might be safe from the sound of the bang in so much as the decibel level produced. But Quietus technically does nothing to counter pressure wave differences which is what blows out ear drums and depending which studies you look at can cause internal injuries when wearing body armor. The way the power is written it only counters half of the issue with explosives. But hey its all made up vampire magic so there you go.<br /> <br /> Poison is a theme, but not a strong one. Namely because writers tend to think only about doing damage with poison and then they get stuck on the rails of only more damage. A better way to look at poison is as a tool kit. Like have one level be a movement debuff, another level being a hallucinogen that makes the target delirious, a different level be a damage boost, then a final level be something really nasty like paralysis or making the victims vitae become inert for a night. Its kind of the difference between the writer who has no idea how poison works verses some one like Paracelsus who understands that any substance can be dangerous it is merely a question of dosage. Thus the true assassination potential comes from a tool kit for a variety of situations rather than variations on just doing damage.<br /> <br /> I can suggest some excellent reference sources if you would like to learn more.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-22T12:18:10-07:00","id":1404071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1403910#post1403910\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">also SoD has a range limit and being so close to mines....</div> </div> </div> </div>Claymores are directional, in case you were wondering. So proximity doesn't work the same way as it does for standard area denial mines.<br /> <br /> For someone casting shade on my players, you are getting a lot wrong about the basics of how the equipment works.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-22T13:08:08-07:00","id":1404076,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1404071#post1404071\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Claymores are directional, in case you were wondering. So proximity doesn't work the same way as it does for standard area denial mines.<br /> <br /> For someone casting shade on my players, you are getting a lot wrong about the basics of how the equipment works.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'll admit bad reading with the claymore issue, I know how they work, I was an idiot there, I'm sorry, relax. <br /> <br /> With the sniping, I was just keeping things simple, and I wasn't saying silencing an anti-material rifle with quietus is a bad idea, just that it's the kind of plan people get overconfident in. Unless you've got multiple snipers or you're picking on a lone target, the lupines will come for you, then or later. Also, septs, i think, have spiritual warning systems and... It's a really good thing that Assamites have a long distance running combo discipline.. <br /> The blood core though? Silly. What use is poison when you have .50bmg? Is the round even going to deliver the toxin before it punches through the victim? And you'd need to poison the bullets on the day; If you're hunting werewolves, just use silver? (I mean poison blood core sounds really cool and all but it's gotta be the least practical option) <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1404069#post1404069\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Poison is a theme, but not a strong one.</div> </div> </div> </div>. <br /> I'm not looking at this from a highly subjective -cool themes to give your character- standpoint; Transforming your blood into various poisons is a very strong theme from a -this is how the magic works- standpoint. It's just as good as controlling the illusionary substance of Chimestry, the telling people how to think of Dominate, the flesh shaping of vicissitude... The simpler you can make your theme, the better, and Blood-Into-Poisons is nice and simple. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-22T14:40:35-07:00","id":1404093,"title":"","content":"How exactly do you guys deal with silenced explosions and the shockwave they produce? I had a recent session where an NPC used quietus to make the blowing of a door silent, but still told the PCs they felt the shockwave from the ground. Since sound is basically just the vibrations like the shockwave traveling through the ground, I could have gone either way on this one, but felt like something that large should still produce some feeling. The people in the building would have known something was going on, but no one at the widely spaced houses in the area would have thought anything of it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14519-drhappyangry","name":"DrHappyAngry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14519_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-22T23:52:57-07:00","id":1404190,"title":"","content":"I think you'd really need to figure in the size of explosion and where you're doing it. <br /> <br /> large bomb with concusive force (not shrapnel) extending beyond the range of quietus? sound is basically normal, perhaps a few decibels down and perhaps a little more abrupt, and perhaps a little better in some directions than others.<br /> <br /> smaller bomb with a shockwave mostly in range, in an appartment? you might feel an indeterminate shudder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 07-23-2020, 03:00 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-23T07:58:21-07:00","id":1404239,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck#post1403866\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Lazy Idea 1:<br /> <b>Blood of destruction</b>.<br /> Super acid poison, like, difficulty 9 to soak, destroys inaminate objects with ease; Carve through safes and warship hulls with this power. Something to that effect. I'm lazy describing it, but I can imagine it'd be pretty versatile. Perhaps it destroys precisely what you want and leaves other stuff untouched.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like this because it seems like something that a vampire assassin would find super-useful for sneaking into locked or secured places (melt the lock, melt the door hinge, etc.). Also used to acid etch the Assamite clan symbol at the site of a successful assassination. Gotta advertise!<br /> <br /> Other random ideas;<br /> <br /> <b>Blood Like Water</b> - curse an enemy so that they can't spend blood for a few turns (1 / success), or, they can spend blood, *but get nothing from it,* just wasting blood! (Mortals just take 1 (unsoakable?) bashing damage every round and become light-headed, as their blood becomes terrible at bringing oxygen to their tissues.)<br /> <br /> <b>Blood Like Fire</b> - A higher powered curse turns your enemies blood to poison, and perhaps, as long as they don't use it, it doesn't burn them, but once they attempt to 'burn blood,' it literally burns like acid in their veins and they have to soak wounds equal to the blood points expended! (Mortals just take 1 agonizing lethal damage every round (-1 dice pool penalty or +1 Diff to rolls from the extra pain?), and don't get an option to 'not spend blood' and avoid harm.)<br /> <br /> <b>Blood Tendrils</b> - you open a wound on yourself (1 unsoakable HT level) and the blood from your body forms a lash that attacks by itself (taking no action on your part once activated, but costing 1 BP / round of use after the first). It has an attack roll using your Dex + Brawl (or Martial Arts or Melee, whatever), and does damage based on your Quietus level (plus extra successes to hit, like most other attacks). The only time this uses a split dice pool is if you activate it *again* and have two blood tendrils going (for 2 BP / round)! The tendrils have your Str score, and could perhaps grapple or even toss others about, if strong enough (range is 1 meter / BP you are capable of spending at your generation, so 1 meter for 13th to 10th Gen, 2 meters long tendrils for 9th Gen, 3 meters long tendrils for 8th Gen!).<br /> <br /> <b>Blood Animus</b> - you animate a BP worth of your own blood and can spit it at close range or deliver it by touch to a person, whereupon it crawls over their face and both blinds and begins to suffocate them. (The latter effect has no affect on vampires, who don't need to breathe, although being blinded can still be a detriment!) Lasts 1 turn / success you roll. Humans take 1 HT level / turn, from 'drowning.' The vitae is transmuted by this effect, and cannot impose a blood bond. (That's not what this is for. You want them *dead,* this discipline is meant to facilitate that. Not making love-slaves!)<br /> <br /> Blood Rituals<br /> 1, you scrawl the text in blood, and it then vanishes, only to reappear when smeared with Assamite blood in the future!<br /> <br /> 2, you feed from someone and put a message *in their blood,* so that when somebody feeds from them, they get a clear mental message (spoken in your voice, in whatever language you 'recorded it in,' so that if the wrong person feeds from this person, they just get a weird babbling in a language that (hopefully!) they don't understand!). For every success on the activation roll, you can implant one 'copy' of this message, so that multiple Kindred could overhear it, if you rolled well, *but* you get to choose whether or not to implant multiple copies, so that if you only want it heard once, and then never again, you can arrange that as well. (Just hope nobody else feeds from this person before the intended recipient!)<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23754-ian-turner","name":"Ian Turner","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"93"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-23T12:05:37-07:00","id":1404274,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1404190#post1404190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you'd really need to figure in the size of explosion and where you're doing it.<br /> <br /> large bomb with concusive force (not shrapnel) extending beyond the range of quietus? sound is basically normal, perhaps a few decibels down and perhaps a little more abrupt, and perhaps a little better in some directions than others.<br /> <br /> smaller bomb with a shockwave mostly in range, in an appartment? you might feel an indeterminate shudder.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's about what I did. Shrapnel still makes noise when it hits stuff outside the affected area, the old house still shakes, you feel a bit of a shockwave through the ground, but they don't realize the full magnitude of what happened unless they saw it. I was curious how others felt about it, since I felt like I was making a judgement call on how that worked.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14519-drhappyangry","name":"DrHappyAngry","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14519_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"233"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-25T12:43:53-07:00","id":1404616,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1404076#post1404076\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With the sniping, I was just keeping things simple, and I wasn't saying silencing an anti-material rifle with quietus is a bad idea, just that it's the kind of plan people get overconfident in. Unless you've got multiple snipers or you're picking on a lone target, the lupines will come for you, then or later. Also, septs, i think, have spiritual warning systems and... It's a really good thing that Assamites have a long distance running combo discipline..<br /> The blood core though? Silly. What use is poison when you have .50bmg? Is the round even going to deliver the toxin before it punches through the victim? And you'd need to poison the bullets on the day; If you're hunting werewolves, just use silver? (I mean poison blood core sounds really cool and all but it's gotta be the least practical option)</div> </div> </div> </div>The thing about werewolfs is that they are deadly in close combat, but not all ranges. When you put a round into a lupines skull from over a mile away, most of their protections don't work or don't have the range required to alert them ahead of time. Further more by the time the other lupines figure out what is happening the shooter is long gone, usually with a few claymores strategically placed to add to the chaos when they close in on the empty firing position.<br /> <br /> The blood core is just something as simple as swapping the chemicals used in a tracer round with a blood capsule. It doesn't really affect the bullet that much, any damage reduction by mass is made up for by damage increase and type by the poison. Like I said the reduction in penetration on a soft target actually means more damage than the round going straight through the target and the next to walls.<br /> <br /> You will notice I specifically mentioned silver talons, contrary to many gaming supplements, silver makes for a horrible bullet material. Silver is harder than lead, copper, or most other bullet materials. The result being that a silver bullet is too hard for the spiral grooves inside the barrel to bite cleanly into the material and force the round to spin. The result being that the bullet &quot;bounces&quot; down the barrel which absolutely kills accuracy, especially at long range. So you use an outer jacket of a softer metal to achieve proper spin, you use silver talons near the core of lead, with a blood capsule near the back of the round. On striking the target the softer and heavier material peels back like a hollow point, this frees up the silver talons to go different directions inside the body, the last thing to enter and interact with the wound is the blood capsule which breaks as it enters the body. The silver does damage as long as it is in the wound, but you can dig it out with some surgery skills, poison on the other hand goes right into the blood stream which is impossible to &quot;dig out&quot;. So yeah, solid silver bullets are highly impractical.<br /> <br /> I get the desire to keep things simple, but gaming is a lot like food. You can only eat so many Mcdonalds dollar burgers before you start wanting a steak and potato dinner with all the fixings. So there is fun to be had being more detail oriented in some aspects of the game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-25T15:08:14-07:00","id":1404647,"title":"","content":"I believe lupines, in lupus form, are going to be very good with running you down. Low profile, fast, improved senses, supiriority to normal wolves in this form, and any gifts they might have; I wouldn't take that shot unless I had a full squad with me waiting in ambush. Shoot and leave is not a good option with lupines, especially considering their tracking capabilities. (That cross country combo sure looks good though) <br /> I fear the exposive charge in the munition would burn away the poison. Vitae and it's poisons are destroyed by fire.<br /> I think you're underselling silver. I looked at it and discussed it a long time ago, and I think the detriments of silver are likely to be rather negligible. Perhaps it might be a concern with extreme distance shooting (Which admitedly, we are discussing) but in that case you could most probably engineer a solution (silver at the front, copper at the sides?).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-28T14:14:33-07:00","id":1405101,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MyWifeIsScary</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1404647#post1404647\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I believe lupines, in lupus form, are going to be very good with running you down. Low profile, fast, improved senses, supiriority to normal wolves in this form, and any gifts they might have; I wouldn't take that shot unless I had a full squad with me waiting in ambush. Shoot and leave is not a good option with lupines, especially considering their tracking capabilities. (That cross country combo sure looks good though).</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes and no.<br /> <br /> The low profile and fast movement only matters if you are shooting at them when they are active, a sniper by definition waits for the circumstances to be right before taking the shot. Meaning the lupine would be stationary, not on guard, and the shape doesn't matter given the precise nature of the shot and the small sizes of targets in training.<br /> <br /> As for improved senses, once again as long as you are down wind and a mile off, they won't smell or hear you. The first clue they will have that you exist is the targets head gaining a new hole. For a couple of minutes everyone is diving for and staying in cover. Next they try to figure out which way the sniper is shooting from, but since no further shots are forth coming, they will scamper from cover to cover out of realistic concern that they could be directly exposing themselves to follow up shots, this can eat up quite a lot of time and hampers their inherent movement benefits.<br /> <br /> How exactly would they track you? Vampires exceed human abilities and the world record for a human sniper shot is 3,871 yards or over two miles away. We have been discussing a shot at only one mile distance. For one mile between the sniper and the target there is nothing to track. At best they could guess that since they can't smell your scent or the gun powder on the wind even with gifts, they can only say you are not directly up wind, since you could be up wind but a mile off to the side. So the wolves would have to search in a grid pattern for almost a mile in every direction and only once they find your sniper blind will they begin to be able to track you, but by then you will have been gone for hours. Especially since the lupines will be searching while trying to keep in cover, which slows things to a crawl.<br /> <br /> As for the gifts themselves, Obfuscate does counter scent, hence why nosferatu can live in the sewers and not be noticed when invisible. Sure they might be able to find your scent and pick up the smell of gunpowder and gun oil, but that will just lead them along your exit path to where ever your get away vehicle or extraction point is, but that doesn't help since as previously stated they are a few hours too late. This becomes even more problematic if the sniper was trained in escape and evasion techniques like running through water or spreading a box of chili powder all over their sniper hide to spoil the scent and mess with the lupines noses.<br /> <br /> To be fair though I will admit that this style of fighting is not for every game, but in truth when you have to fight, you fight to win, not to fight fair.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-29T01:06:56-07:00","id":1405139,"title":"","content":"I Don't hate it. I've seen players use it and I've had considerable success myself through the equally dirty art of IED's. I think a problem lies in the fact that you need a perfect scenario: You need to know where the lupines will loiter, you need the wind on your side, you need the right terrain, you need to know that you're shooting at lupines and not people or wolves, Also the lupine might Rage back to life (if you use WTA rules), meaning you'll need two shots and two turns to put them down (Yes, even if you shoot through the head)...<br /> <br /> A single lupine is rare, but Werewolves travel in packs. Kill one and the rest will, after a short 'oh shit' moment where they activate their gifts, observe the gnarly exit wound, and come for you. If the lupines have the right gifts*, and you don't have backup, you will be in for a desperate fight. If the lupines don't come at you immediately, there's also a good chance they'll have a thirst for vengance and will try to use their abilities (mundane and supernatural) to hunt you later.<br /> <br /> *seriously; Lupines can pick up some absolute nightmare powers for your sniper strategy if you use WTA rules. You'll get that first kill, but after that....<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;Typically vampires<br /> using Obfuscate must be within a short range of<br /> their witnesses (approximately five yards or meters per<br /> dot of Wits + Stealth) for their power to be effective.&quot;<br /> So, you can't smell the sewer on someone within that range, but if the smell was detected outside of that range and followed in range....<br /> Not to mention that I am unsure if obfuscate really metaphysically works on someone who is actively hunting <i>specifically you</i>. Obfuscate is a mental trick; it doesn't delete stimuli, it just fools people in range into not sensing the stimuli.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Anyhow. Quietus:<br /> <br /> If I made an absurdly cool, not imbalanced, -totally not saying anything cause it's worth storyteller's vaulting- super thematically appropriate power for quietus 1/quietus in general, Should I keep SoD, a classic, as an additional Quietus 1 power? (meaning you get both, which might be very strong). Auspex 1 grants people three different powers essentially, this'd be similar.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-29T02:42:48-07:00","id":1405148,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar25794_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"25794\" data-vbnamecard=\"25794\">MyWifeIsScary</a> My first question to you would be - what is the purpose of Quietus to you? As a designer. Mechanically and narratively, what is Quietus meant to be?<br /> <br /> There are two obvious answers, assuming we don't want to redo Quietus too much. The first is that Quietus is the assassination discipline. The second is that Quietus is a blood manipulation discipline.<br /> <br /> For an assassination discipline, we are keeping Silence of Death and keep designing in this area. An off the top of my head sketch:<br /> 1. <b>Blessing of Elil</b>, Lore of the Clans Vizier version of Silence of Death - you project a sphere of silence but can choose to exclude things from it. It's magical silence so objects that left the sphere are still silent (such as bullets).<br /> 2. <b>Ishtar's Touch.</b> Bite a target (without taking any of their blood), spend a blood point, roll Stamina + Intimidation. For the rest of the scene you gain +Successes in dice to your mundane social checks against the target. This is your &quot;Solid Snake holds the target at gunpoint and questions them for intel&quot; power.<br /> 3. <b>Utu's Lock.</b> Rub some blood on an object, the next person to touch it takes damage. Now by level 3 we have a solid &quot;assassin&quot; package that lets a chargen PC be an enjoyable Tactical Espionage Action protagonist.<br /> 4. <b>Nergal's Call.</b> Aka Dagon's Call, but with an assyrian name. Untraceable remote attack. Fine as is.<br /> 5. <b>Ashur's Road.</b> Spend a blood point to transform into a stream of blood for a turn, along with all your equipment. This can be done to pass under a door, or as a defence action in combat to avoid an attack.<br /> <br /> For a blood manipulation discipline. I don't have cool names, you'd have to figure out your own. I would also use this to replace the Tremere Path of Blood, because it's kinda not great:<br /> 1. <b>Haemomancy.</b> Touch blood, roll Willpower, learn one fact per success related to the target's health, physical condition or character.<br /> 2. <b>Scarlet Shepherd. </b>Vampire learns the direction and general distance of blood sources sympathetically connected to a sample. Vampire themselves is connected to their herd, their ghouls and any vitae they have spilled recently.<br /> 3. <b>Theft of Vitae.</b> Theft of Vitae.<br /> 4. <b>Haemothurgy</b>. Spend a blood point to control blood in unnatural ways, such as moving it or forming combat tentacles (like Arms of Abyss). The blood needs to be already present, so if it's not - you'll have to bleed yourself some more.<br /> 5. <b>Puppetmaster.</b> Make a roll, control target's actions for a turn per success. The target doesn't <i>know</i> they are being controlled, but unless you are performing very minor actions they will realize it's not just some accidental hand twitching. This can be used to force vampires to activate disciplines they know (and this can be subtle, such as activating Dominate on a sentence that vampire is saying of free will). This is very spooky to other vampires and cause for great distrust..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","user":"Kammerer","date":". 07-29-2020, 07:38 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12892-kammerer","name":"Kammerer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12892_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1191"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-29T04:31:30-07:00","id":1405150,"title":"","content":"It's been a while since I discussed this, but my interpretation of Disciplines in general is that they aren't immutable laws of supernatural reality, that only work because that's the only way they could ever work.<br /> <br /> Disciplines are a set of powers discovered and refined by their inventors - usually by the first three Generations - meant for particular purposes. Their inventors wanted something out of the powers, and the sets of them we encounter are the ones that got used so often as to become ingrained in the Blood itself.<br /> <br /> <i>(Obviously, I'm talking V20 and earlier here. With V5, this goes right out the window.)</i><br /> <br /> This is why, say, Serpentis is a Discipline built around becoming more snakey. Set really liked snakes, and so most of the early Serpentis powers are designed to facilitate this. However, Set also wanted to be worshiped as a god, which is why later powers began picking up abilities that facilitate looking like a god, or having storm powers. It's a toolset that formed for Set's purposes, and changed as the needs of Setites changed. (This is why in DAV20, you had Serpentis 5 being used to produce monsters, and Serpentis 4 created a more dramatic &quot;war form&quot;. Meanwhile, by modern day, Serpentis 4 merely turned you into a big snake (because it's more subtle than a person with a snake lower half or a typhonic beast), and Serpentis 5 made what was once a Ritual into a standard power known even by non-blood sorcerers).<br /> <br /> <br /> For Quietus, I'm much more inclined to go with &quot;Quietus is the assassination Discipline&quot;, rather than &quot;Quietus is the blood manipulation Discipline&quot;. If it's a constructed powerset, then its theme would need to be less fundamental, and more about what Haqim (or his Warrior followers) needed it for. In this case, <i>Assassination</i>. That its most basic power lets you sneak better has nothing to do with whether it's blood-related, and everything to do with &quot;sound canceling&quot; just being <i>really</i> useful for an assassin to have. To the point that it was developed in the same skillset as other assassination powers, even when Assamites had access to Obfuscate, a more dedicated &quot;stealth&quot; Discipline. It was THAT important to have in the &quot;Quietus package&quot;.<br /> <br /> Hell, most vampires would appreciate being able to move silently.<br /> <br /> <br /> To reiterate, this is just my interpretation of how Disciplines work. If your vision for what they are is different, we may never see eye to eye on this.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":6156,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","user":"Bluecho","date":". 07-29-2020, 08:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-29T18:09:15-07:00","id":1405223,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Draconis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1403891#post1403891\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Random musings:<br /> <br /> So, my biggest issues with Quietus have always been that the first power has no thematic connection to the others (as mentioned), and that the powers feel\u2026redundant. Turn your blood into poison, turn your blood into <i>better</i> poison, turn your blood into more versatile poison\u2026<br /> <br /> Some other clan-specific disciplines have the same issues (like Vicissitude, which is three powers that all do the same thing to different extents, and two unrelated Protean powers). And I'm not sure it's something that can be entirely fixed within V20's paradigm. Maybe a good model would be Chimerstry, which does a good job of making the enhancements feel meaningful, imo: each one feels like a significant upgrade over Ignis Fatuus in and of itself, but comes with a significant cost that means you won't want to use it every time. But &amp;quot;poisonous blood&amp;quot; is a very narrow space to work within.<br /> <br /> Maybe borrow some ideas from the Tremere's Path of Blood? &amp;quot;Manipulate properties of vitae&amp;quot; is a larger thematic space to explore than &amp;quot;your blood is poison&amp;quot;. Take some of the elder abilities, like &amp;quot;inflict the drawbacks of high Generation on someone&amp;quot;, and move them down into the easily-accessible 1-5 progression; mix them with variants on some of the Tremere abilities, like stealing vitae from a distance, which could be an enhancement to Dagon's Call. And after all, the Banu Haqim were following the Path of Blood for thousands of years before the Tremere showed up. (That last one is a joke, if it wasn't clear.)</div> </div> </div> </div>I rewrote most of the base Disciplines for an online v20 game set in Vegas that I plan on running soon, and this is basically the exact route I took with Quietus.<br /> <br /> Here are my 1-5. Some of them are adjusted elder-level or v20DA powers, some of them were lifted from other homebrews that I found online, and some were my own creation. P.S: this may seem a little OP to some of you, but the vast majority of Disciplines in my game have trended towards being buffed rather than nerfed, so it is fairly balanced in comparison:<br /> <br /> Level 1 - Scent of Vitae<br /> Eons ago, the Banu Haqim served as both the judges and executioners of the earliest Cainite societies, and even the Clan\u2019s neonates were capable of hunting down anyone who dared to break the Laws of Caine. At its most basic levels, Quietus allows a vampire to identify the source of Cainite vitae by scent, gleaning information about the bleeder\u2019s Clan, age, and Generation. This power also allows the user to determine if a person is a Cainite, ghoul, or mortal.<br /> System: The user spends a blood point and for the remainder of the scene, she may identify the source of any amount of blood by scent by making a Perception + Awareness roll. This can be used on a pool of blood to identify if any Cainites contributed to it, on a stranger to determine if that person is a ghoul, mortal, or Cainite, and on a Cainite\u2019s blood to determine their age, Generation, and Clan.<br /> Note: this power does not increase the user\u2019s sense of smell, per se, it simply allows her to identify the unique scent of Cainite vitae. Any blood samples or targets must be close enough to sniff, and the user cannot track someone like a bloodhound without the use of Protean.<br /> <br /> None/Botch: The power fails<br /> 1 Success: The user can determine if a small blood sample is composed of vampiric vitae, in part or in whole.<br /> 2 Successes: The user can determine if a person is a mortal, ghoul, or Cainite with a sniff, and will know if she has smelled that person\u2019s blood before.<br /> 3 Successes: The user can determine the age of a Cainite whose blood is sampled.<br /> 4 Successes: The user can determine the Clan of a Cainite whose blood is sampled.<br /> 5+ Successes: The user can determine the Generation of the Cainite whose blood is sampled, and possibly other information as determined by the number of successes and the Storyteller.<br /> <br /> <br /> Level 2 - Mark of Guilt<br /> Using this power, the vampire marks a target both with her blood and with the judgment of Caine. This can take the form of a bloody handprint from an enemy combatant, or a subtle smear from the midst of a crowd. For as long as the victim is marked with the user\u2019s blood, he is unable to spend blood points, as his vitae is continually being used up in the form of a grisly blood-sweat.<br /> Originally used by the Banu Haqim of antiquity when performing interrogations and dealing with Cainite prisoners; the loss of blood and rising hunger of the target, while useful as a bargaining chip, was mostly secondary to the benefit of blocking the use of vampiric Disciplines. In the modern nights, a number of other useful applications have arisen. For example: ensuring that a business deal is performed without the use of any mind-altering Disciplines, or humiliating a social rival in the city\u2019s Elysium by making them ruin their outfit while tempting Frenzy.<br /> System: The user first spends a blood point and applies it to her target through physical contact, which can require a Dexterity + Brawl roll, like a normal attack. For the remainder of the scene, or until the target removes the mark, he will sweat out his blood at a rate equal to his own Generational maximum, or 3 blood points per turn if used on a mortal. This counts as a vampire\u2019s blood spent for that turn, effectively locking the target out of their blood-activated Disciplines, vampiric healing abilities, and Attribute augmentation. This power lasts until the target is able to remove the mark, which can be done with a single action. If used outside of combat, the victim must succeed at a Perception + Awareness roll (difficulty 7) to notice the mark.<br /> Blood lost through this process is considered dead and inert, providing no sustenance to a vampire. It could, however, inspire frenzy in hungry Cainites. While this power isn\u2019t necessarily a Masquerade breach in and of itself, its effects are quite dramatic and will likely cause a panic in an area filled with mortals. Obviously, if used on a mortal, Mark of Guilt can be fatal, depending on how long they are affected.<br /> <br /> Level 3 - Dagon\u2019s Call<br /> With the slightest scratch delivering a dab of blood to mingle with that of the target\u2019s, Dagon\u2019s Call twists a victim\u2019s vitae against him. The target\u2019s eyes swell and take a deep burgundy hue, muscles seize and veins bulge beneath the skin, and thin trickles of vibrant scarlet bleed into tiny rivers from all openings in the anatomy. This nightmarish affliction persists until the target collapses in agony or death throes, leaving no trace of the attacker\u2019s presence.<br /> System: In order to use this power, the character\u2019s blood must first be ingested or enter the target\u2019s bloodstream, usually through drinking (even from a ghoul belonging to the character) or being pierced by a bloodied weapon. The character waits a minimum of one hour for her blood to be fully absorbed, entering the circulatory system to securely grip the victim\u2019s internal organs.<br /> After a moment\u2019s concentration, the vampire bursts her target\u2019s blood vessels, internally constricting the target\u2019s whole body by flooding it with ruptured hemoglobin that strangles him from within. To activate this power, the player spends one Willpower point and rolls Perception + Awareness (difficulty equal up to the Storyteller, depending on the vampire\u2019s current proximity to the target or the amount of time elapsed since she touched the target, whichever is greater). Failure on this roll means that the Willpower point is wasted, and the target must be exposed to the user\u2019s blood again.<br /> The player and target make contested Stamina rolls (difficulty of each roll equal to the opponent\u2019s Willpower). The target suffers levels of unsoakable lethal damage equal to the number of successes on the player\u2019s Willpower roll minus successes on the target\u2019s Willpower roll. To continue rending her opponent from within, the player may spend additional Willpower each turn after the first, accompanied by further contested Stamina rolls, until she is defeated on a roll. A botched roll results in the vampire suffering an amount of lethal damage equal to her failures on the roll.<br /> <br /> Level 4 - Baal\u2019s Caress<br /> The ultimate use of blood as a weapon (short of diablerie itself), Baal\u2019s Caress allows a Kindred to transmute her blood into a virulent ichor that destroys any living or undead flesh it touches. In nights of yore, when Assamites led the charges of Saracen legions, the Assassins were often seen licking their blades, slicing open their tongues and lubricating their weapons with this foul secretion. Baal\u2019s Caress may be used to augment any bladed weapon; everything from poisoned knives and swords to tainted fingernails and claws has been reported. Additionally, the Cainite may spit their corrosive blood at distant targets or vomit up a torrent of corrosive bile upon their victims<br /> System: Baal\u2019s Caress does not increase the damage done by a given weapon, but that weapon inflicts aggravated damage rather than whatever kind of damage it would normally deal. No roll is necessary to activate this power, but the vampire must coat their weapon in a number of blood points up to their Generational maximum. This may be done over multiple turns, adding the blood points to the weapon\u2019s total each time. One blood point is consumed per hit. For example, if a Cainite poisons his knife with 3 blood points and strikes his opponent, one blood point\u2019s worth of lubrication disappears, leaving enough for two more attacks. For this reason, many vampires choose to coat their weapons with a significant quantity of blood before they enter combat. If the vampire misses, no tainted blood is consumed.<br /> A vampire may also bite her tongue or lip to spit a single point of envenomed blood at a lone target, or regurgitate up to her maximum amount of blood point expenditure per turn to hit multiple targets. The Cainite may spit or vomit her envenomed vitae up to three feet (one meter) for each dot of Strength and Potence she possesses. Spitting at a lone target requires a successful Dexterity + Athletics roll (difficulty 6) to hit, and vomit may be sprayed to hit multiple targets at the Storyteller\u2019s discretion. Spewed blood contacting the victim\u2019s skin causes two levels of Aggravated damage per blood point spent. If the blood is ingested, this damage is unsoakable, even with Fortitude.<br /> <br /> Level 5 - Thinning of the Blood<br /> Much as its title suggests, this level of mastery of Quietus allows its user to temporarily raise the Generation of a Cainite by focusing her will and touching her target\u2019s skin. The target immediately feels the power of the Blood of Caine slip away and is effectively reduced to the status of a thin-blooded vampire. More effort is required to activate his Disciplines, his vitae cannot sustain others, and his will can be more easily crushed by vampires who possess more powerful blood.<br /> Of course, this power can be useful in combat, as the target must spend far more blood to achieve formerly simple vampiric tasks, but it also prevents a vampire from sustaining his ghouls or siring childer. While the effects of this power do wear off after a number of nights, intelligently timed or repeated applications can wreak havoc on a well-established vampire\u2019s sphere of influence. Previously enthralled subjects may rebel, desert, or even hunt their former masters during the day as their blood bonds fade to reveal resentment and hatred.<br /> System: The user must spend a Willpower point, make a Willpower roll (at a difficulty equal to the target\u2019s Temporary Willpower,) and touch her target\u2019s skin. After contact, all of the target\u2019s blood point costs are doubled (e.g., using blood-related Disciplines or healing damage,) although he still only loses one blood upon rising in the evening. Furthermore, he is unable to create or maintain a blood bond, cannot sire new vampires, and is treated as a 14th Generation vampire for any Generation-dependent effects.<br /> These effects last a number of nights equal to double the number of successes that the user scored in the initial activation of this power. No successes means that the effect only lasts until sunrise. On a botch, the user is affected instead, also lasting until sunrise.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25404,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25404-justanothercaitiff","user":"JustAnotherCaitiff","date":". 07-29-2020, 09:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25404-justanothercaitiff","name":"JustAnotherCaitiff","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25404_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"21"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2020-07-30T03:22:35-07:00","id":1405287,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>JustAnotherCaitiff</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1403866-making-quietus-not-suck?p=1405223#post1405223\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Here are my 1-5. Some of them are adjusted elder-level or v20DA powers, some of them were lifted from other homebrews that I found online, and some were my own creation. P.S: this may seem a little OP to some of you, but the vast majority of Disciplines in my game have trended towards being buffed rather than nerfed, so it is fairly balanced in comparison: <br /> Level 1 - Scent of Vitae<br /> Level 2 - Mark of Guilt<br /> Level 3 - Dagon\u2019s Call<br /> Level 4 - Baal\u2019s Caress<br /> Level 5 - Thinning of the Blood</div> </div> </div> </div>I like. Potent, yes, but a good source of ideas, even if one wants to tweak the exact mechanics. I like Mark of Guilt and Thinning of the Blood, particularly.<br /> <br /> Inspired by Mark of Guilt, a couple of ideas;<br /> <br /> A) Sanguine Exchange - You touch your enemy and draw forth a number of blood points immediately that are transmuted and can be used to activate disciplines, temporarily raise your physical attributes, or heal injury (even if such a use would normally require a separate action). Any not immediately used are not lost by the target, so it's in your best interest if you wish to weaken someone more fully to burn as much blood as you can activating Celerity, healing wounds and enhancing all of your physical attributes as high as you can. You can draw forth 1 BP / success on the roll, and this number is not limited to the amount of blood you can spend per turn based on your Generation (and, indeed, you may also have spent blood normally up to your /turn limit, before using this ability). Because the blood is immediately transmuted and 'burned,' and not absorbed into your body, there is no risk of Blood Bond, or suffering a similar risk (such as from a bloodborne contagion) from this power's use.<br /> <br /> B) Bound by Scarlet - You link your vitae to another with a touch, for weal or woe. You can spend your own vitae to heal their wounds, or enhance their physical attributes, or activate Disciplines such as Celerity, so long as they remain within ten paces. The effect lasts for the scene, unless you choose to end it earlier. You can also interfere with their own use of their Vitae, if they are a vampire. You spend 1 Blood point per turn you wish to 'block' them, and they have to spend 1 Willpower to access their blood for the turn, or else be blocked entirely from spending blood.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23754-ian-turner","name":"Ian Turner","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"93"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}